"Quasi-Kernel (https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Quasi-Kernel.html)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; 2026).\n\nThe small quasi-kernel conjecture asserts that every source-free directed graph on Image: n vertices has a quasi-kernel of size at most Image: n/2.\n\n# Quasi-Kernel\n\nA quasi-kernel of a directed graph is an independent vertex set from which every graph vertex is reachable by a directed graph path of length at most two. This convention directs paths away from the quasi-kernel. The reverse convention is obtained by reversing all directed edges (Ai et al. 2026).\n\nThe small quasi-kernel conjecture asserts that every source-free directed graph on Image: n vertices has a quasi-kernel of size at most Image: n/2. Source-free means that every graph vertex has positive indegree. The large quasi-kernel conjecture asserts that every directed graph has a quasi-kernel whose closed out-neighborhood contains at least half its vertices. This neighborhood consists of the quasi-kernel and all vertices reached from it by one directed edge. Ai et al. (2026) proved that these two universal conjectures are equivalent. Neither is thereby proved. Their conventions allow opposite directed edges but exclude graph loops and parallel directed edges.\n\n## See also\n\nDirected Graph, Indegree, Independent Vertex Set\n\n## References\n\nAi, J.; Gutin, G.; Liu, X.; and Meng, X. \"Small and Large Quasi-Kernels: A Weighted Transfer Theorem.\" 16 Oct 2026. https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23487.\n\n## Cite this as:\n\nWeisstein, Eric W. \"Quasi-Kernel.\" From MathWorld--A Wolfram Resource. https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Quasi-Kernel.html--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA 24-Vertex Oriented Graph Showing f(4) ≥ 5/4 in a Source-Neighborhood Problem for Quasi-Kernels · AFTD (https://rainwangphy.github.io/aftd-platform/blog/2026-11-03-arxiv-2601-11847/)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 days ago; Crawled: 5 days ago; Every digraph has one, and the Small Quasi-Kernel Conjecture asserts that every sourceless digraph on $n$n vertices has one of size at most $n/2$n/2.\n\nThe paper arXiv:2601.11847 approaches the conjecture through a smallest counterexample.\n## 1. Introduction\n\nA quasi-kernel of a digraph $D$D is an independent set $Q$Q such that every vertex is reached from $Q$Q by a directed path of length at most two. Every digraph has one, and the Small Quasi-Kernel Conjecture asserts that every sourceless digraph on $n$n vertices has one of size at most $n/2$n/2.\n\nThe paper arXiv:2601.11847 approaches the conjecture through a smallest counterexample. For a vertex $v$v, deleting the closed out-neighbourhood $N^+[v] = \\{v\\} \\cup N^+(v)$N+[v]={v}∪N+(v) may create sources; write $S(v)$S(v) for the set of sources of $D - N^+[v]$D−N+[v] and $$ E(v) = N^+(v) \\cup N^+(S(v)). $$E(v)=N+(v)∪N+(S(v)). Its Algorithm 1 builds a quasi-kernel from a vertex $v$v and a quasi-kernel of a smaller sourceless digraph, and the resulting criterion is: if every sourceless digraph has a vertex with $t|E(v)| \\ge |S(v)| + 1$t∣E(v)∣≥∣S(v)∣+1, quasi-kernels of size at most $tn/(t+1)$t n/(t+1) follow, so $t = 1$t=1 would give the conjecture. The paper proves a $4n/7$4 n/7 bound for maximum out-degree three this way, conjectures (Conjecture 5.1) that every sourceless oriented graph of maximum out-degree at most three has a vertex with $|E(v)| \\ge |S(v)| + 1$∣E(v)∣≥∣S(v)∣+1, and shows (Proposition 2.3) that without a degree bound no constant works: for every $c > 0$c>0 there is a sourceless digraph in which every non-sink vertex has $c|E(v)| < |S(v)| + 1$c∣E(v)∣<∣S(v)∣+1.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSmall Quasi-Kernel Conjecture; Large Quasi-Kernel Conjecture | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/408938/small-quasi-kernel-conjecture-large-quasi-kernel-conjecture)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; The small conjecture, attributed to Erdős and Székely in 1976, asserts that every sink-free digraph on $n$n vertices has a quasi-kernel of size at most $n/2$n/2. ...   * Ai, Liu, and Peng (2024): the small conjecture implies the large one; both remain open, with further bounds for kernel-perfect digraphs.\n\nNew work improves the best known bounds for both conjectures, but neither conjecture has been resolved.\n\nThe small conjecture, attributed to Erdős and Székely in 1976, asserts that every sink-free digraph on $n$n vertices has a quasi-kernel of size at most $n/2$n/2. The large conjecture asks for a quasi-kernel $Q$Q whose closed in-neighborhood satisfies $|N^-[Q]|\\ge n/2$∣N−[Q]∣≥n/2; both remain open in general.\n\nKnown results\n\n  * Chvátal and Lovász (1974): every digraph has a quasi-kernel.\n  * Kostochka, Luo, and Shan (2022): the small conjecture holds for several restricted classes, including orientations of $4$4-colorable graphs.\n  * Ai, Gerke, Gutin, Yeo, and Zhou (2023): the small conjecture holds for generalized anti-claw-free digraphs, including sink-free one-way split digraphs.\n  * Ai, Liu, and Peng (2024): the small conjecture implies the large one; both remain open, with further bounds for kernel-perfect digraphs.\n\nOctober 2026 advance\n\nDaniel Rutschmann's article reports an improved upper bound for small quasi-kernels and a new coverage estimate for the large conjecture, moving both results toward their conjectured linear-scale conclusions. This is claimed progress, not a resolution, and the mathematical claims have not been independently assessed here.\n\nCurrent status (as of October 2026): Both conjectures remain open; an October 2026 article claims improved bounds for each, but those advances are unverified.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThere are many interesting open questions about *k*-kernels. For instance, not every digraph has a 1-kernel (the cyclic triangle is a counterexample), but Chvátal and Lovász [3] showed that every digraph has a 2-kernel. Every 2-kernel must contain every source (a source is a vertex with in-degree zero), but if there are no sources, how large must 2-kernels be in terms of the number of vertices? (We write \\|G\\| for \\|V(G)\\|.) The following was conjectured by P. L. Erdős and L. A. Székely [5] in 1976, and remains open: (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v33i1p32/pdf/)\nciteturn2search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; **Conjecture 1.1** (The small quasi-kernel conjecture). ... If \\(G\\) is a counterexample to Conjecture 1.1, then since it has a 2-kernel, it has a stable set \\(S\\) with \\(|S| > |G|/2\\); and a natural special case is when \\(G \\setminus S\\) is a tournament. ... It seems that the best known bound in this case is due to Spiro, in the same paper, who proved that under the hypotheses of Conjecture 1.4, there is a *k*-kernel of size at most about \\(|G|/\\log k\\). ... THE ELECTRONIC JOURNAL OF COMBINATORICS 33(1) (2026), #P1.32\nThere are many interesting open questions about *k*-kernels. For instance, not every digraph has a 1-kernel (the cyclic triangle is a counterexample), but Chvátal and Lovász [3] showed that every digraph has a 2-kernel. Every 2-kernel must contain every source (a source is a vertex with in-degree zero), but if there are no sources, how large must 2-kernels be in terms of the number of vertices? (We write \\|G\\| for \\|V(G)\\|.) The following was conjectured by P. L. Erdős and L. A. Székely [5] in 1976, and remains open:\n\n**Conjecture 1.1** (The small quasi-kernel conjecture). Every digraph \\(G\\) with no source has a 2-kernel of size at most \\(|G|/2\\).\n\nThere has been extensive work proving special cases of the conjecture and exploring related problems (see, for example, [1, 2, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 12]; and see [4] for a survey). The best general bound seems to be a result of Spiro [12], that every digraph \\(G\\) with no source has a 2-kernel of size at most \\(|G| - \\frac{1}{4}(|G|\\log |G|)^{1/2}\\), which is of course very far from the conjecture. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWeighted Half-Coverage by Quasi-Kernels in Oriented Graphs Whose Non-Neighborhoods Have Kernels · AFTD (https://rainwangphy.github.io/aftd-platform/blog/2026-11-04-op-337/)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 days ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; medium tier 2026-11-05 OP-337 reviewed by Claude Code中文版 ... Hence every source-free oriented graph of the class satisfies the Small Quasi-kernel Conjecture; this deduction rests on the paper's theorem and is not part of the Lean development. ... Two small formalized examples mark the limits of simpler strategies: in `digraph_seven_tournament_two_neighborhood_cover_counterexample`, a source-free tournament on 7 vertices has every singleton as a quasi-kernel and only singletons, yet no two of their closed out-neighborhoods cover all vertices; in `digraph_small_quasikernel_max_independent_cover_counterexample`, a source-free oriented graph on 7 vertices has a maximum independent set of size 4 that lies in the closed out-neighborhood of no quasi-kernel of size at most $7/2$7/2.\n\nBy Theorem 1.3 of arXiv:2610.23487, every source-free digraph with the weighted half-coverage property has a quasi-kernel of size at most $\\lfloor |V|/2 \\rfloor$⌊∣V∣/2⌋. Hence every source-free oriented graph of the class satisfies the Small Quasi-kernel Conjecture; this deduction rests on the paper's theorem and is not part of the Lean development. For oriented complete multipartite graphs the half-order bound itself was already known (arXiv:2610.23487 recalls that semicomplete multipartite digraphs have two disjoint quasi-kernels); there the theorem adds the weighted statement. The class is incomparable with that of break digraphs, so the result extends the reach of the weighted method beyond the classes treated in the paper without containing them.\n\nTwo small formalized examples mark the limits of simpler strategies: in `digraph_seven_tournament_two_neighborhood_cover_counterexample`, a source-free tournament on 7 vertices has every singleton as a quasi-kernel and only singletons, yet no two of their closed out-neighborhoods cover all vertices; in `digraph_small_quasikernel_max_independent_cover_counterexample`, a source-free oriented graph on 7 vertices has a maximum independent set of size 4 that lies in the closed out-neighborhood of no quasi-kernel of size at most $7/2$7/2.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGreedily Constructing Small Quasi-Kernels (https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.11847)\nciteturn2academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Date: Sat Jan 17 00:34:18 2026 ... We introduce a polynomial time algorithm which greedily constructs a small quasi-kernel. ... Finally, we consider the problem in digraphs forbidding certain orientation of short cycles as subgraphs, concluding that all orientations $D$ of a graph $G$ with girth at least $7$ have a quasi-kernel of order at most $\\frac{(d^2+4)n}{(d+2)^2}$, where $d$ is the maximum out-degree of $D$.\nTitle: Greedily Constructing Small Quasi-Kernels\nAuthors: Alexander Clow\nDate: Sat Jan 17 00:34:18 2026\n\nIn a digraph $D$,a quasi-kernel is an independent set $Q$ such that for every vertex $u$, there is a vertex $v \\in Q$ satisfying $\\text{dist}(v,u)\\leq 2$. In 1974 Chvátal and Lovász showed every digraph contains a quasi-kernel. In 1976, P. L. Erdős and Székely conjectured that every sourceless digraph has a quasi-kernel of order at most $\\frac{n}{2}$. Despite significant recent attention by the community the problem remains far from solved, with no bound of the form $(1-ε)n$ known. We introduce a polynomial time algorithm which greedily constructs a small quasi-kernel. Using this algorithm we show that if $D$ is a $\\vec{K}_{1,d}$-free digraph, then $D$ has a quasi-kernel of order at most $\\frac{(d^2 - 2d + 2)n}{d^2-d+1}$. By refining this argument we prove that for any $D$ with maximum out-degree $3$ this algorithm constructs a quasi-kernel of order at most ${4n}/{7}$. Finally, we consider the problem in digraphs forbidding certain orientation of short cycles as subgraphs, concluding that all orientations $D$ of a graph $G$ with girth at least $7$ have a quasi-kernel of order at most $\\frac{(d^2+4)n}{(d+2)^2}$, where $d$ is the maximum out-degree of $D$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChapter 7\n\n# Complexity\n\nSections 7.1, 7.2, and 7. (https://helene-langlois.alwaysdata.net/perso/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/Manuscrit-39.pdf)\nciteturn2search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.5 years ago; Sections 7.1, 7.2, and 7.3 form a paper that has been published at the conference WG 2022 [60]. ... The small quasi-kernel conjecture leads to other natural questions such as the complexity of finding a smallest quasi-kernel or of finding two disjoint quasi-kernels. ... In 2004, the same authors constructed a counterexample to this stronger conjecture with 14 vertices [48] depicted in Figure 7.1.\nChapter 7\n\n# Complexity\n\nSections 7.1, 7.2, and 7.3 form a paper that has been published at the conference WG 2022 [60]. Section 7.5 results from a collaboration with Julien Baste and Antoine Castillon.\n\nIn this chapter, we focus on the size of quasi-kernels in digraphs with an algorithmic point of view. Indeed, contrary to kernels, the existence of quasi-kernels is systematic. The question about the size of quasi-kernels is then very natural. The small quasi-kernel conjecture leads to other natural questions such as the complexity of finding a smallest quasi-kernel or of finding two disjoint quasi-kernels. We prove that the problem is computationally hard even for small classes of graphs. Finally we provide polynomial algorithms to find a smallest quasi-kernel and disjoint quasi-kernels in orientations of bounded treewidth.\n\n## 7.1 Disjoint quasi-kernels\n\nIn 2011 Gutin et al. [47] conjectured that every sink-free digraph has two disjoint quasi-kernels. This conjecture implies the small quasi-kernel conjecture, since if a digraph has two disjoint quasi-kernels, at least one has less than half of the vertices of the digraph. In 2004, the same authors constructed a counterexample to this stronger conjecture with 14 vertices [48] depicted in Figure 7.1. As the following theorem proves, not only sink-free digraphs occasionally fail to contain two disjoint quasi-kernels, but it is actually computationally hard to distinguish those that do from those that do not.\n\nTheorem 7.1.1. *Deciding if a digraph has two disjoint quasi-kernels is NP-complete, even for digraphs with maximum outdegree six.*\n\nFigure 7.1: A split digraph having no two disjoint quasi-kernels.\n\n59--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTowards the Small Quasi-Kernel Conjecture (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v29i3p49/pdf)\nciteturn2search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.2 years ago; Proof of Theorem 6\nAssume Conjecture 3 fails and D is a counterexample to it with the fewest vertices. ... |V (D)| −1, the minimality of n implies that D1 has a quasi-kernel K1 of size at most ... the electronic journal of combinatorics 29(3) (2022), #P3.49\nTherefore K′ is a quasi-kernel of\nD.\n3\nProof of Theorem 6\nAssume Conjecture 3 fails and D is a counterexample to it with the fewest vertices.\nLet n = |V (D)|. We assume n ⩾4 as the cases n ⩽3 are veriﬁable by hand. By the\nminimality of n, D is weakly connected. Let S be the set of sources of D. We show that\nS = ∅. Assume instead that S ̸= ∅.\nCase 1: |N +\nD[S]| ⩾3. Let D1 be obtained from D by deleting all vertices in N +\nD[S],\nadding two new vertices x and y, adding an arc from y to every vertex of D −N +\nD[S] that\nis an outneighbor of some vertex of N +\nD(S) in D, and adding an arc from x to y.